alex: Heads-up — streaming client in prod is intermittently stalling. Pattern looks like partial JSON arriving split across SSE frames and the client tries to parse halfway through. Anyone seen this recently? repro + logs below.

alex: repro (simplified):
```js
const es = new EventSource(url);
let buf = '';
es.onmessage = (e) => {
  buf += e.data;
  // naive parse:
  try {
    const obj = JSON.parse(buf);
    handle(obj);
    buf = '';
  } catch (err) {
    // JSON.parse: Unexpected end of JSON input
  }
};
```

priya: Yup. Saw this in the web chat client — heavy small-message traffic + emoji split across utf8 boundaries made JSON.parse blow up. We switched to an incremental parser and a small delimiter on the server (ndjson) as a mitigation. PR: https://git.redwood/pr/streaming-ndjson-sample :thumbsup:

matt: Also check auth rotation. If the client refreshes token mid-stream it can trigger a reconnect that lands mid-object on the consumer. Are you rotating bearer tokens frequently?

alex: We refresh tokens every 10m via background refresh. There are a few 401s in the traces but stalls happen even without explicit 401. Attaching short trace snippet: 

alex: LOGS:
[3205559876.100] recv chunk len=48 data="{\"evt\":\"m\",\"p\":\"partial\""]
[3205559876.104] recv chunk len=22 data="-payload\"}"
[3205559876.105] JSON.parse error: Unexpected end of JSON input
[3205559876.106] client wait for more chunks... stall 1.2s

olga: Two quick options: server-side emit explicit delimiters (ndjson/newline) so clients can parse per-line, or client-side use a streaming JSON parser that can handle partial tokens. Also consider reducing server batch size or emitting smaller, self-contained events to avoid splits.

priya: We used `clarinet` on node and a tiny stateful parser in the browser. For the browser implementation the trick was to treat `\n` as the object delimiter. Also added a tiny keepalive 1-byte ping at intervals so the connection doesn't idle.

matt: For backpressure we experimented with a simple "window" header where client sends current in-flight allowance and server respects it, throttling batch composition. Cut client stalls during load spikes. Not perfect but helped.

devex-bot: FYI docs: https://go.redwood.dev/streaming-guide — includes recommended client parsers and server ndjson option. Test harness: https://ci/streaming-replay. I can run a replay if you drop a trace link.

alex: Plan proposal: 1) add opt-in ndjson output for streaming endpoints, 2) add a browser sample showing incremental parse + buffer logic, 3) add telemetry to detect partial-JSON parse failures and surface as a warning. I'll open a PR and a replay ticket. Assigning review to @priya.

priya: I'll review tomorrow. Quick Q: default NDJSON on by default? That could break existing clients.

olga: Make it opt-in initially, log a deprecation warning or telemetry, then flip after adoption.

alex: Agreed. Opt-in + telemetry. PR by EOD. tagging @matt to prototype server windowing change.

matt: On it — I'll prototype server-side batch-size throttle and a `X-Client-Window` header check today.

